      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a report for a vineyard client in the Central Valley. Can you explain how soil pH levels in the 5.5 to 7.0 range specifically impact the availability of micronutrients like iron and manganese for grapevines? Need to explain the solubility aspect.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the 5.5 to 7.0 pH range, iron is typically the more limiting micronutrient due to phytosiderophores, small, soluble iron-binding compounds produced by grapevines. These compounds chelate limited available iron, reducing its bioavailability and mobility throughout the soil. Manganese, on the other hand, is more readily available and has less influence from phytosiderophores, even at lower pH levels. Therefore, ensuring adequate levels of iron in this pH range is crucial to supporting optimal grapevine growth.",
